  "tour": {
    "title": "Hesed: More Than One English Word",
    "intro": "This tour starts with the translation problem, then follows hesed through Exodus, family loyalty, royal generosity, prophetic critique, and the Psalms. You’ll hear why “steadfast love,” “covenant faithfulness,” “mercy,” and “loving-kindness” each catch part of the word—while no single gloss captures all of it.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "recordId": "bibleproject:podcast:loyal-love-god",
        "startSec": 0,
        "endSec": 180,
        "why": "Begin here for the central definition and the reason translations vary. The speaker describes hesed as combining “being generous with being loyal” and “deep affection,” then adds that it involves a covenant partner doing “concrete acts” to fulfill a promise. The clip also names the major English renderings—“mercy,” “loving kindness,” “love,” and “steadfast love”—without treating any one of them as a complete equivalent.",
        "episodeTitle": "The Loyal Love of God",
        "sourceId": "bibleproject",
        "source": "BibleProject",
        "audioUrl": "https://afp-597195-injected.calisto.simplecastaudio.com/695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee/episodes/e530ba3f-428a-4ef5-828c-34800da4963f/audio/128/default.mp3?awCollectionId=695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee&awEpisodeId=e530ba3f-428a-4ef5-828c-34800da4963f",
        "durationSec": 4105,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "forty-minutes-ot:podcast:c416445833e65b5f726afb158ec92353",
        "startSec": 1370,
        "endSec": 1620,
        "why": "Now hear the word anchored in its key Old Testament setting: God’s self-description in Exodus 34. After reading the passage, the speaker explains that hesed means “goodness in action”—not merely an inward emotion, but love that goes outward “into this act of goodness.” This also keeps the definition from becoming sentimental: the clip notes that God forgives while still not simply winking at sin.",
        "episodeTitle": "Episode 105 (Exodus 33:18-34:16)",
        "sourceId": "forty-minutes-ot",
        "source": "40 Minutes in the Old Testament",
        "audioUrl": "https://adbarker.com/stream/tfVE3XBxC9JCmb97VsZt7vpT/traffic.libsyn.com/secure/40minot/40_Min_ep_105.mp3?dest-id=245197",
        "durationSec": 2488,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "bibleproject:podcast:loyal-love-god",
        "startSec": 1940,
        "endSec": 2070,
        "why": "Ruth shows that hesed is not restricted to God’s actions or to formal legal language. Naomi says, “May Yahweh do chesed with you, as you’ve shown chesed to me,” and Ruth answers with her costly vow: “Where you go, I go.” The teachers call this an act of hesed because Ruth offers familial and covenant loyalty when Naomi has “nothing to give her,” making the generosity and commitment dimensions visible.",
        "episodeTitle": "The Loyal Love of God",
        "sourceId": "bibleproject",
        "source": "BibleProject",
        "audioUrl": "https://afp-597195-injected.calisto.simplecastaudio.com/695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee/episodes/e530ba3f-428a-4ef5-828c-34800da4963f/audio/128/default.mp3?awCollectionId=695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee&awEpisodeId=e530ba3f-428a-4ef5-828c-34800da4963f",
        "durationSec": 4105,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "forty-minutes-ot:podcast:478e6896-2d24-4806-ac5f-4db234766229",
        "startSec": 1630,
        "endSec": 2010,
        "why": "This longer narrative gives the word another shape: David’s hesed toward Mephibosheth, the disabled grandson of his political enemy. The speaker says David is acting as “the instrument of Yahweh” to show covenant loyalty, and the scene lands in David’s promise: “Do not fear,” followed by restored land and a permanent place at the king’s table. Hesed here is loyal love expressed through surprising, concrete generosity toward a vulnerable rival.",
        "episodeTitle": "2 Samuel 8:9-9:13 (Episode 267)",
        "sourceId": "forty-minutes-ot",
        "source": "40 Minutes in the Old Testament",
        "audioUrl": "https://adbarker.com/stream/tfVE3XBxC9JCmb97VsZt7vpT/traffic.libsyn.com/secure/40minot/40_Min_ep_667.mp3?dest-id=245197",
        "durationSec": 2471,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "spoken-gospel:podcast:633264c5-ede2-4f20-abae-f6b16f3d5c4d",
        "startSec": 700,
        "endSec": 830,
        "why": "Hosea 6:6 shows the prophetic and ethical range of hesed. The familiar translation is “I desire mercy, not sacrifice,” but the speaker identifies the Hebrew as “God’s covenant, steadfast, faithful, loyal love.” The point is not to discard sacrifice; it is to expose worship severed from relationship—“all the sacrifice, but none of the love.” This helps you see why hesed can be translated as mercy in context while still meaning more than pity.",
        "episodeTitle": "Hosea 5:8-11:11: Politics and Religion",
        "sourceId": "spoken-gospel",
        "source": "Spoken Gospel",
        "audioUrl": "https://traffic.megaphone.fm/SPG8402683449.mp3?updated=1664486091",
        "durationSec": 3757,
        "adInsertionDrift": true
      },
      {
        "recordId": "forty-minutes-ot:podcast:a07922e45da2445b8c36defa6d82cc67",
        "startSec": 1460,
        "endSec": 1640,
        "why": "Finish with Psalm 136, where hesed becomes the refrain interpreting Israel’s whole story. The speaker identifies “for his loving kindness is everlasting” as the repeated line through all twenty-six verses, covering creation, the exodus, and entry into the land. The conclusion is a helpful reading principle: the Psalm makes the “loving kindness of Yahweh” the interpretive key for the Torah, showing hesed as enduring action across generations rather than a single moment of kindness.",
        "episodeTitle": "Live From HWSS 2019",
        "sourceId": "forty-minutes-ot",
        "source": "40 Minutes in the Old Testament",
        "audioUrl": "https://adbarker.com/stream/tfVE3XBxC9JCmb97VsZt7vpT/traffic.libsyn.com/secure/40minot/40_Min_HWSS.mp3?dest-id=245197",
        "durationSec": 2510,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      }
    ],
    "closing": "Taken together, these clips suggest that hesed is not literally impossible to translate; it is unusually rich. In any given verse, context may foreground mercy, kindness, loyalty, faithfulness, affection, forgiveness, or generosity. But the fuller range is a committed relationship expressed in concrete, generous action—especially action that keeps faith when there is no obvious repayment.",
    "totalSeconds": 1250
  },
